Arrays Basic
==========================================================================================================================
1. Largest Element in an Array
Example: arr = {3, 2, 1, 5, 2} → 5
Brute force: Sort the array and return the last element.
Arrays.sort(arr);
return arr[arr.length - 1];


Time: \(O(n\log n)\) · Space: depends on sorting implementation.
Better: Not needed separately; a linear scan is already optimal.
Optimal: Traverse once and track the maximum.
int max = arr[0];

for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > max) {
        max = arr[i];
    }
}
return max;


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
2. Second Largest Element Without Sorting
Example: {10, 5, 20, 8} → 10
Brute force: Sort, then scan backward to find an element smaller than the maximum.
Arrays.sort(arr);
int n = arr.length;
int largest = arr[n - 1];

for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (arr[i] < largest) return arr[i];
}
return -1;


Time: \(O(n\log n)\).
Better: Find the largest first, then scan again for the largest value smaller than it.
Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
Optimal: Maintain the largest and second-largest distinct values in one traversal.
int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
int second = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

for (int x : arr) {
    if (x > largest) {
        second = largest;
        largest = x;
    } else if (x < largest && x > second) {
        second = x;
    }
}

return second == Integer.MIN_VALUE ? -1 : second;


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
Note: For a fully general implementation, use a separate boolean flag to distinguish a missing second-largest element from a legitimate Integer.MIN_VALUE value.
3. Check Whether an Array Is Sorted
Brute force: Compare every pair \(i<j\); if arr[i] > arr[j], it is not sorted.
Time: \(O(n^2)\).
Better: Compare adjacent elements.
Optimal: The adjacent comparison is already optimal.
boolean isSorted(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
4. Remove Duplicates from a Sorted Array
Example: {1, 1, 2, 2, 3} → unique prefix {1, 2, 3}.
Brute force: Insert elements into a HashSet.
Set<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();
for (int x : arr) set.add(x);


Time: \(O(n)\) expected · Space: \(O(n)\).
Better: Use a temporary array to store distinct elements.
Time: \(O(n)\) · Space: \(O(n)\).
Optimal: Two pointers, modifying the original array.
int removeDuplicates(int[] arr) {
    if (arr.length == 0) return 0;

    int j = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] != arr[j]) {
            arr[++j] = arr[i];
        }
    }
    return j + 1;
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
The first j + 1 elements contain the unique values.
5. Left Rotate an Array by One Place
Example: {1, 2, 3, 4} → {2, 3, 4, 1}.
Brute force: Create a new array and copy elements from index 1 onward, then append the first element.
Time: \(O(n)\) · Space: \(O(n)\).
Better / Optimal: Save the first element, shift everything left, and put the saved element at the end.
void rotateLeft(int[] arr) {
    if (arr.length == 0) return;

    int first = arr[0];

    for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
        arr[i - 1] = arr[i];
    }

    arr[arr.length - 1] = first;
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
6. Left Rotate an Array by D Places
Example: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, d = 2 → {3, 4, 5, 1, 2}.
Brute force: Rotate left by one place, repeated d times.
Time: \(O(nd)\) · Space: \(O(1)\).
Better: Store the first d elements in a temporary array and shift the remaining elements.
Time: \(O(n)\) · Space: \(O(d)\).
Optimal: Use the reversal algorithm.
void reverse(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
    while (left < right) {
        int temp = arr[left];
        arr[left] = arr[right];
        arr[right] = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
}

void rotateLeft(int[] arr, int d) {
    int n = arr.length;
    if (n == 0) return;

    d %= n;

    reverse(arr, 0, d - 1);
    reverse(arr, d, n - 1);
    reverse(arr, 0, n - 1);
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
7. Move Zeros to the End
Example: {1, 0, 2, 0, 3} → {1, 2, 3, 0, 0}.
Brute force: Store nonzero elements in a temporary array, then fill remaining positions with zeros.
Time: \(O(n)\) · Space: \(O(n)\).
Better / Optimal: Use a pointer to track the next position for a nonzero element.
void moveZeros(int[] arr) {
    int j = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] != 0) {
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[j];
            arr[j] = temp;
            j++;
        }
    }
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
8. Linear Search
Find the index of a target in an unsorted array.
Brute force / Better / Optimal: All use the same linear scan; no asymptotically faster general method exists for an unsorted array.
int search(int[] arr, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == target) return i;
    }
    return -1;
}


Time: \(O(n)\) worst case · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
9. Find the Union of Two Sorted Arrays
The union contains each distinct value from either array.
Brute force: Combine the arrays, sort, and remove duplicates.
Time: \(O((n+m)\log(n+m))\).
Better: Use a set to combine unique elements.
Time: \(O(n+m)\) expected · Space: \(O(n+m)\).
Optimal: Use two pointers because both arrays are sorted.
List<Integer> union(int[] a, int[] b) {
    List<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
    int i = 0, j = 0;

    while (i < a.length || j < b.length) {
        int x;

        if (j == b.length ||
            (i < a.length && a[i] < b[j])) {
            x = a[i++];
        } else if (i == a.length || b[j] < a[i]) {
            x = b[j++];
        } else {
            x = a[i];
            i++;
            j++;
        }

        if (ans.isEmpty() || ans.get(ans.size() - 1) != x) {
            ans.add(x);
        }
    }
    return ans;
}


Time: \(O(n+m)\) · Output space: \(O(n+m)\).
10. Find the Missing Number
For an array containing distinct values from 0 to n, with one value missing.
Brute force: For every number from 0 to n, search the array.
Time: \(O(n^2)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
Better: Use a frequency array to mark present numbers.
Time: \(O(n)\) · Space: \(O(n)\).
Optimal: Use the sum formula.
int missingNumber(int[] arr, int n) {
    long expected = (long) n * (n + 1) / 2;
    long actual = 0;

    for (int x : arr) actual += x;

    return (int) (expected - actual);
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
Another optimal option is XOR, which avoids sum overflow.
11. Maximum Consecutive Ones
Example: {1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1} → 3.
Brute force: Examine every starting position and count consecutive ones.
Time: \(O(n^2)\).
Better / Optimal: Maintain the current streak and maximum streak.
int findMaxConsecutiveOnes(int[] arr) {
    int count = 0, max = 0;

    for (int x : arr) {
        if (x == 1) {
            count++;
            max = Math.max(max, count);
        } else {
            count = 0;
        }
    }
    return max;
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
12. Find the Number That Appears Once; Others Appear Twice
Example: {4, 1, 2, 1, 2} → 4.
Brute force: Count the occurrences of every element.
Time: \(O(n^2)\).
Better: Use a HashMap to count occurrences.
Time: \(O(n)\) expected · Space: \(O(n)\).
Optimal: XOR all the elements. Equal pairs cancel out.
int singleNumber(int[] arr) {
    int xor = 0;

    for (int x : arr) {
        xor ^= x;
    }
    return xor;
}


Time: \(O(n)\) · Extra space: \(O(1)\).
13. Longest Subarray With Sum K — Positive Numbers
=================================================
sliding window is used when K value is given

1. Brute Force Approach
=======================
Idea: Generate every possible subarray and calculate its sum.
Time: O(n²) · Extra space: O(1)

class Solution {
    public int longestSubarray(int[] arr, int k) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int sum = 0;

            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                sum += arr[j];

                if (sum == k) {
                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, j - i + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return maxLen;
    }
}

Using HashMap
